-- title:
--   Adelic box of a lattice is cut out place by place
-- statement:
--   Let $D$ be a ring equipped with a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra structure, and let $\Lambda$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of $D$ which is finitely generated and whose $\mathbb{Q}$-span, as a subset of $D$, is all of $D$; thus $\Lambda$ is a full $\mathbb{Z}$-lattice in $D$. Let $x$ be an element of $D \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{f}$, where $\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{f}$ is the finite adele ring of $\mathbb{Q}$ with respect to the ring of integers $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$. The assertion is an equivalence. On one side, $x$ belongs to [`Submodule.finiteAdeleBox`](def/Submodule_FiniteAdeleBox.html#L14) $\Lambda$, the additive subgroup of $D \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{f}$ generated by the elementary tensors $z \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} a$ with $z \in \Lambda$ and $a$ a finite adele all of whose components $a_v$ lie in the valuation ring $\mathcal{O}_v$ of the $v$-adic completion of $\mathbb{Q}$. On the other, for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ the image of $x$ under the $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra map $\mathrm{id}_D \otimes (\text{evaluation at } v) : D \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{f} \to D \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{Q}_v$ belongs to [`Submodule.localBox`](def/Submodule_LocalBox.html#L40) $\Lambda$ $v$, the additive subgroup of $D \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{Q}_v$ generated by the tensors $z \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} c$ with $z \in \Lambda$ and $c \in \mathcal{O}_v$.
--
--   This is the local–global dictionary for completions of a full lattice $\Lambda$ in a finite-dimensional $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra: $\widehat{\Lambda} = (D \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{f}) \cap \prod_v \Lambda_v$, expressed here in terms of the additive subgroups generated by integral elementary tensors. It serves as the basic membership criterion in the adelic vocabulary used by the Čerednik–Drinfeld tower computations with Eichler orders, where $\Lambda$ is an order in a quaternion algebra over $\mathbb{Q}$.
